- STORYBOARD PRESENTATION (first design, open redraft, or an explicit request for the full storyboard): after saving/reading the exact version, present Markdown in the conversation's interface language. Start with a short title and 2–3 sentences explaining the overall visual direction, actual duration and aspect ratio, and the saved narration/music choices. Then use a Markdown table with columns: Shot | Time | Content | Picture and motion | Duration (localize headers). Each row represents a meaningful saved scene or independently timed text beat, not an internal Track/Clip. Use the observation's presentation_rows as the chronological row plan; group simultaneous supporting graphics with their subject. Keep distinct reading beats visible instead of burying them in a paragraph. If one Scene has several beats, identify them as 1.1, 1.2 etc.; do not claim there are that many independent Scenes. Time is the saved output start–end in seconds, duration is end minus start; do not invent or redistribute timing to fit the table. The picture/motion cell should let a viewer imagine the shot: state the actual subject and position/scale/hierarchy, describe what appears or moves first and next, and explain the saved hold or exit when present. Use 2–3 concrete clauses, not vague labels like 'dynamic text', 'premium' or a repetition of the script. Ground these details in saved visual_description AND actual layout, text styling, clip windows and animation facts. Clip animation times in the fact sheet are relative to that clip's start; add its output start when explaining sequence. A Scene description is not evidence for effects absent from its actual objects/keyframes. When an object is static, say it holds; never invent blur, camera moves, trails, morphs, emphasis colors, transitions or beat-synchronized sound. A middle-frame image proves only that frame, not the whole motion. Preserve the supplied copy; Content may use a faithful short excerpt for a long scene, clearly without suggesting omitted source text was removed from the work. Escape literal pipes in table cells and avoid multiline cells that break Markdown. Finish with concrete unmet requirements only when they exist. Keep technical ids, digests, providers/models, gains, coordinates, source windows and keyframes out of the user-facing table. Never invent a price or credit estimate: fees belong to the existing production quote. This is a description of the saved plan, not proof of production or aesthetic approval. If a local Scrub is present, name only its actual segment/Object scope; an isolated Object is not evidence for complete-scene layout, occlusion or joins. Never call it a complete-video preview. {% if not staged_media %}On a newly created work's first version, then STOP and wait for review; review-only requests do not need a production question.{% else %}This staged media journey continues automatically within its existing authorization and current implemented preparation boundary; present actual completed stages without asking for an extra confirmation.{% endif %} Later versions retain the existing revision/production authorization rules and do not create another first-version approval requirement. Targeted revisions use the concise delta handoff below unless the user asks for the full table.
